# Do and Don’t Lists

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A Do and Don’t Lists pair puts advice in two short lists: what to do, then what not to do. It suits a help page for looking after a dashboard or checking an integration, where each piece of advice is short and the two lists answer the same question.

Home Assistant has no element for this. It is a Civica pattern, built from a heading and a list for each side. A single instruction needs only a paragraph, [Inset text](inset-text.html) or an [alert](alert-and-tip-callout.html).

This page covers:

- Do and Don’t Lists
  - Civica Do and Don’t Lists Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

1. A “Do” section: a heading, then a list of actions.
2. A “Do not” section: a heading, then a list of cautions.
3. A tick beside each Do item and a cross beside each Do not item. They are decorative and hidden from assistive technology.
4. A new heading after the lists, so later content is not read as more advice.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-do-and-don-t-lists-pattern"><code>Civica Do and Don’t Lists Pattern</code></h3>

Each section has a thin border and padding. Each item is a row with the tick or cross, then the text, with a gap between rows. The tick uses the success colour and the cross uses the error colour. In the Civica theme these are `success-color` (`#0F7A52` in light mode, `#BCDACF` in dark mode) and `error-color` (`#CA3535` light, `#F0C6C6` dark). The border uses `divider-color` (`#CECECE` light, `#50565A` dark), and the text uses `primary-text-color`.

A single Do not list is fine when there is no useful opposing set.

In a Home Assistant Markdown card you can write the two headed lists, with “Do” and “Do not” in the headings. The card does not give you the bordered sections or the tick and cross graphics, so the words carry everything:

```markdown
## Do
- Use room names the household recognises.
- Check the dashboard on a small screen.

## Do not
- Do not remove the text that explains an unavailable device.
- Do not use colour as the only status indicator.
```

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Two short lists are quick to scan, and the headings give each list a name.
- The text carries the meaning, so the pair works without the graphics and without colour.
- The pair is not a native Home Assistant element, so a dashboard needs the Markdown version above.
- The graphics and borders are not available in a Markdown card.
- Long advice does not fit: a list of more than a few short items becomes a page of its own.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Write advice the reader can act on, and keep it short, with one verb at the start of each item. Keep device-specific servicing instructions in the maker’s own guidance.

- Use real headings and real lists, so the structure is announced.
- The text of every Do not item contains “do not”, so it stays correct when read without its heading.
- Mark the tick and cross as decorative. Colour only supports the words.
- Put Do first, then Do not, in the same order every time. Stack the two lists; do not place them side by side, because the reading order becomes unclear.
- Do not turn status values into advice. A state such as “Unavailable” belongs in the [wording rules for states](../content/writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html), not in a Do not list.
- Do not put essential validation or consent in this pattern.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: real headings and lists.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the words carry the meaning.
- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: decorative graphics are hidden.
- 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence: Do then Do not, stacked.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the lists stack at 320 CSS pixels.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: each list has a clear heading.

Test the spoken order, the stacked layout on a small screen and the page with colour removed. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

